## Report
Dynamic attempts: `cargo test -p server-core resolve_new_object_lock_state_allows_past_retention -- --nocapture` failed to build because the `ec-sys` build script could not find `libisal` ("libisal not found; install intel-isa-l or set PKG_CONFIG_PATH"), so no runtime PoC could be executed. `valgrind` and `gdb` were not installed in the container.

Code review evidence: the write-path header parser constructs `ObjectRetention` directly from `x-amz-object-lock-mode` and `x-amz-object-lock-retain-until-date` without checking the timestamp against the current time or bucket defaults (crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:4501-4554). The retain-until header parser only validates timestamp format, not whether it is in the future (crates/server-http/src/http/xml.rs:2441-2444). On the core side, `validate_requested_object_lock_state` only checks whether Object Lock is enabled for the bucket (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:6585-6594). `resolve_new_object_lock_state` applies the bucket’s default retention only when the request has no retention; explicit retention is accepted verbatim (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:6597-6618). This means a client can supply a retain-until timestamp in the past and bypass default retention by having the server store an already-expired retention value.

