# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Although the logic flaw is real and reachable via normal S3 write operations, it requires authenticated write access to an Object Lock-enabled bucket and does not grant new read privileges or cross-tenant access. The impact is a bypass of retention policy for objects written by the attacker, which is serious for integrity/compliance but narrower than typical high-severity authz bypass or data exfiltration. Therefore the severity is adjusted from high to medium based on constrained scope and preconditions.
## Likelihood
high - Exploit requires valid write credentials and a bucket with Object Lock enabled, but is straightforward once those preconditions are met (send past retain-until header). | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - Bypassing default retention allows premature deletion or overwrite of objects that should be WORM-protected, undermining integrity/availability guarantees for Object Lock buckets.
## Assumptions
- The argmin-s3 HTTP service is reachable by the attacker (ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR may be bound to a non-loopback interface).
- A bucket is configured with Object Lock enabled and default retention.
- The attacker has valid S3 credentials with write permissions to the target bucket.
- Authenticated S3 write credentials
- Bucket with Object Lock enabled (default retention configured)
- Ability to send x-amz-object-lock-mode and x-amz-object-lock-retain-until-date headers
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4 -> n5
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:4501-4554` - Object Lock headers are parsed and ObjectRetention is created directly from header values without validating retain-until semantics.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/xml.rs:2437-2446` - Header timestamp parser validates only format; no check that retain-until is in the future.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:6597-6618` - resolve_new_object_lock_state applies default retention only when none is provided; explicit retention is accepted verbatim.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:1-70` - Service listens on configurable ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR (default 127.0.0.1:9000), indicating network-exposed HTTP surface when configured.
## Narrative
The HTTP layer parses Object Lock headers and constructs ObjectRetention without checking whether the retain-until timestamp is in the future. The timestamp parser only validates format. The coordinator resolves the new object lock state by applying bucket defaults only when no retention is provided, so an explicit past retain-until is stored verbatim. An authenticated writer can therefore submit a past retain-until date to create objects whose retention is already expired, bypassing the bucket’s default retention policy and allowing deletion/overwrite earlier than intended.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication required for write operations
- Bucket Object Lock must be enabled for retention to apply
- Versioning enforced for Object Lock buckets
## Blindspots
- No runtime PoC due to build failures (missing libisal); static analysis only.
- Actual deployment exposure (public vs internal) depends on ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR configuration not present in repo.
- Retention enforcement on delete/overwrite is inferred from design; full end-to-end behavior not exercised dynamically.
